topic: "A university open day",
audioText: "Good morning, everyone, and welcome to the open day at Westbridge University. My name is Dilnoza Karimova, and I will be your guide until lunchtime. First, a quick change to the printed programme. The leaflet says the welcome talk begins at half past nine, but because of a delay with the coaches it will start at ten thirty instead, so you have a little time for coffee. The talk about engineering has been moved to room fifteen, not room fifty, which is in a completely different building, so please check the signs carefully.\n\nMany of you have asked about fees. For students from this country the tuition is nine thousand pounds a year, but there is a bursary for anyone whose family income is below twenty-five thousand. I should add that the bursary is not automatic. You have to apply before the first of March, and last year several excellent candidates missed it because they thought it was included. Accommodation is guaranteed for first-year students, although I have to be honest, the older halls are noisy, and I personally would recommend the new building by the river, even though it costs about forty pounds more per week.\n\nNow, about the courses. I know that some of you came expecting to see the law department, but unfortunately it is closed today because of building work, so you will only be able to meet its staff at the lunch in the main hall. Medicine is not offered here at all, whatever the website might suggest. The old page was out of date, and we are trying to remove it. On the other hand, our psychology course has just been ranked among the top five in the country, which surprised even us.\n\nWhen I was a student here, I nearly left after the first term. I had chosen economics because my father wanted me to, and I found the lectures dull. A tutor persuaded me to switch to geography, and that decision changed my life. So my advice is simple: do not choose a course only because it sounds impressive. Visit the lectures, talk to current students, and ask the awkward questions. There are twelve student ambassadors in red jackets around the campus, and they will answer anything honestly, even about the food, which, I admit, is not our greatest strength.\n\nFinally, the library tour leaves at noon from the front steps, and the last bus back to the station departs at four forty-five, not five o'clock as it did in previous years. Enjoy your day.",
