topic: "A lecture on budgeting",
audioText: "Good afternoon. Today I want to talk about something that most of you will have to face very soon, namely how to manage your money when you live away from home for the first time. I will not pretend that I always did it well. In my first year I spent nearly my whole loan in the first two months, and I had to ask my aunt for help, which I still remember with embarrassment.\n\nThe method I would like to explain is called the fifty, thirty, twenty rule. Fifty percent of your income goes on needs, such as rent, food and transport. Thirty percent is for wants, meaning entertainment, clothes and travel. The remaining twenty percent is for savings or paying off debts. Some of you will say that it is impossible when rent alone takes sixty percent. I agree, and that is the first lesson, a rule is only a starting point. If your needs are higher, you should reduce the wants rather than the savings, even if that feels unfair.\n\nThe second idea is to track your spending for one month. Do not change anything at first, just write it down. Most people are shocked. One student I taught calculated that she was paying thirteen pounds a month for subscriptions she had forgotten about, and she cancelled them the same evening. Another found that coffee on the way to lectures cost him about thirty pounds a month, which is not a fortune, but over a year it adds up to a good holiday. I am not saying that you must give up coffee. I am saying that you should choose it consciously.\n\nThird, build an emergency fund. The usual advice is to save three months of expenses, but for a student that is unrealistic, so I suggest a smaller target of five hundred pounds. That is enough to cover a broken laptop or an unexpected train ticket home. Keep it in a separate account, so you are not tempted to touch it.\n\nNow, a common mistake. Many people believe that using a credit card is always dangerous. It is not, if you pay off the whole balance every month. The danger is paying only the minimum, because the interest can reach twenty-five percent a year. On the other hand, I would not advise any of you to take a payday loan under any circumstances. Those have no exceptions in my book.\n\nLast week a student asked me whether budgeting makes life boring. My answer is the opposite. When you know exactly how much you can spend, you enjoy spending it without guilt. Next week we will look at investing, but I warn you, I will be sceptical about anything that promises quick profits. Please read the first two chapters before then.",
