topic: "An interview with a scholarship winner",
audioText: "Today in our education programme we meet Aziza Nazarova, who has just won a full scholarship to study marine biology in Scotland. The presenter begins by congratulating her and asking how she is feeling. Aziza laughs and says that she is still in shock, because she did not expect to win at all. She had applied three times. The first two applications were rejected, and she admits that after the second she nearly gave up.\n\nThe presenter asks what changed the third time. Aziza explains that in the first year she wrote a very general essay about loving the sea, which, as she now understands, was exactly what everyone else wrote. In the second year her grades were not high enough, because she had spent too much time on volunteering. The third time she combined the two, using her volunteer work cleaning beaches as evidence of a long-term commitment, and she raised her marks to ninety-two percent. She says that the interview panel asked her more about the beaches than about her exam results.\n\nThe presenter then asks about money, because many listeners worry that scholarships are only for the very rich or the very clever. Aziza disagrees. Her family is not wealthy. Her father drives a taxi and her mother works in a school canteen, and the scholarship covers tuition, accommodation and a monthly allowance of eight hundred pounds. She stresses, however, that it does not cover flights, so she had to raise two thousand pounds herself. A local company helped, and neighbours organised a charity concert, which, she says, moved her to tears.\n\nWhen asked about the hardest part, Aziza does not mention the application. It was the waiting. For six weeks after the interview she checked her email every hour, and she says her mother told her to stop, but she could not. She also tells a funny story about the day the news arrived. She was in the middle of a chemistry lesson, her phone buzzed, and the teacher, who had banned phones, asked her to leave. Outside, she read the message and screamed so loudly that the whole corridor heard.\n\nFinally, the presenter asks for advice. Aziza says three things. First, apply early, because the deadline is not a friend. Second, ask someone to read your essay who will tell you the truth, not just praise. Third, never think that a rejection means you are not good enough. In her case, she says, the rejections were the best teachers she ever had. The presenter thanks her, and Aziza leaves for the airport next month, though she is already packing.",
