topic: "A flat viewing",
audioText: "Zilola and her friend Mark arrive at a small block of flats near the park at four o'clock, and the agent, a young man called Peter, is already waiting at the door. He apologises for the lift, which is out of order, and says that they will have to climb to the third floor. Zilola does not mind, but Mark, who is carrying a heavy bag, sighs loudly.\n\nInside, the flat is bright, and the living room faces south. Peter explains that the rent is six hundred and fifty pounds a month, and that the advertisement, which said five hundred and sixty, contained a mistake. Zilola is a little annoyed, because she had planned her budget around the lower number. Peter says the owner might accept six hundred if she signs for a full year, but he cannot promise anything. Zilola replies that she will think about it, although she says she is not hopeful.\n\nThe kitchen is small but modern, with a new oven and a washing machine. However, there is no dishwasher, and Peter says the owner will not install one. In the bedroom there is a large built-in wardrobe, which Zilola likes very much, but the window looks onto a busy road. She asks whether it is noisy at night. Peter says that it is quiet after eleven, though Mark points out that a lorry has just passed and the glass trembled. Peter smiles uncomfortably and says that double glazing will be fitted next spring.\n\nThe agent then goes through the conditions. The deposit is equal to one month's rent, and it is returned at the end of the contract if nothing is damaged. Pets are not allowed, except for fish, which Mark finds funny. Bills are not included, apart from water, so Zilola must also pay for electricity and the internet. The shortest contract is six months, and she can leave early only if she gives two months' notice.\n\nOn the way down, Zilola tells Mark that she has mixed feelings. She loves the light and the wardrobe, and the park is only five minutes away. But the price is more than she wanted, and she is worried about the noise. Mark advises her to visit again in the evening, which she thinks is a good idea. Before they leave, she asks Peter whether there is another flat available in the same building. He says there is one on the first floor, slightly smaller, for six hundred pounds, but it will not be free until next month. Zilola says she will call him on Thursday with her decision, and she asks him not to show the flat to anyone else before then, if possible.",
