topic: "A parent-teacher meeting",
audioText: "On Thursday evening Gulnora Ergasheva goes to her son's school to meet his class teacher, Mr Patel. Her son Timur is fourteen, and she has been worried about him since the autumn. She sits down, thanks the teacher for his time, and says she hopes the news is not too bad.\n\nMr Patel smiles and says that, on the whole, it is better than she thinks. He begins with mathematics, where Timur has improved a great deal. At the start of the year he was getting forty percent in tests, but his last result was seventy-five. The teacher believes that the extra lessons on Tuesday afternoons have made the difference. Gulnora is delighted and says she was not sure whether Timur was even going to those lessons. Mr Patel confirms that he has missed only one.\n\nHowever, there are problems in other subjects. In history Timur has handed in only three of the seven homework tasks, and the last essay was copied from the internet. The teacher says he is not accusing Timur of cheating on purpose, because he might not understand that copying is wrong, but he must learn quickly. Gulnora is embarrassed and promises to speak to him. She asks whether he should be punished at school. Mr Patel says no, not this time, but a second case would mean a meeting with the head teacher.\n\nThen they talk about behaviour. Timur is generally polite, and the other pupils like him, but he talks too much in class, especially when he sits next to his friend Jasur. The teacher has decided to move them to different desks, and he hopes this will help without any further action. Gulnora says she is not surprised, because Timur talks all the time at home, too, unless he is looking at his phone.\n\nGulnora raises the subject of next year. Timur says he wants to study computer science, and she wonders whether he should drop art to concentrate on maths and physics. Mr Patel disagrees. He says art is the one subject in which Timur is truly relaxed and creative, and universities like to see a wide range of interests. He suggests keeping art but dropping geography, in which Timur has no interest and gets only average marks.\n\nAt the end, the teacher gives her a list of three websites for practising mathematics and asks her to limit his phone use to one hour on school nights. Gulnora promises to try. She says she will come again in March, not in June as she did last year, because she wants to be sure that the history problem has been solved.",
