topic: "A talk on student loans",
audioText: "Thank you for coming, everyone. I have been giving this talk for eight years, and it is always the same. Half of you look worried, and the other half look bored. Let me say right now that the bored half should listen very carefully, because student loans are not as simple as they appear.\n\nFirst, the basics. A student loan is money that you borrow to pay for tuition and living costs. Unlike a bank loan, you do not start paying it back until you earn above a certain amount. At present that amount is twenty-seven thousand pounds a year. If you earn less, you pay nothing, and if you earn more, you pay nine percent of the part above the threshold, not nine percent of your whole salary. Many students misunderstand this. So a graduate earning thirty thousand pounds pays nine percent of three thousand pounds, which is two hundred and seventy pounds a year, or about twenty-two pounds a month.\n\nSecond, interest. The loan grows while you are studying, and this is the part that frightens people. A student who borrows forty thousand pounds may owe fifty thousand by graduation. But, and this is important, whether you ever repay all of it depends mainly on your salary, not on the size of the debt. After thirty years, any balance remaining is cancelled. For a lot of average earners that means that they will never pay it all back, so in practice the loan behaves more like a tax than a debt.\n\nThird, a warning. This system applies to home students only. If you are an international student, you cannot get this kind of loan, and you will need a private lender, whose conditions are very different. Private loans have to be repaid in full, with no cancellation, and the rate may change. I would be careful there. Several students who sat where you are sitting now borrowed privately and regretted it.\n\nSome of you will ask whether it is better to work during your degree instead of borrowing. My honest answer is that a few hours a week is useful, but more than fifteen hours often damages your marks. I have seen students lose a whole grade because they worked every evening. And whatever you do, do not take extra credit for things you do not need, such as the latest phone.\n\nNow, questions. I see a hand at the back. Yes, about the thirty years. Does it start from graduation or from the first payment? It starts in the April after you finish your course, even if you were unemployed at the time. I will finish by saying that borrowing for education is rarely a mistake, but borrowing without understanding is.",
