topic: "A wedding planning conversation",
audioText: "Bobur and his fiancée Nigora sit down with a wedding planner called Mrs Ahmedova on a rainy Tuesday morning. They have been engaged for six months, and their families have strong opinions about everything. Mrs Ahmedova opens her notebook and asks them to begin with the number of guests.\n\nBobur says that they have written a list of one hundred and fifty people, but Nigora immediately disagrees. She says that, after talking with her mother, the list has grown to one hundred and eighty, because of distant relatives who would be offended if they were not invited. Bobur is surprised and says that he never agreed to this, and that they cannot afford it. Mrs Ahmedova suggests a compromise. A hundred and twenty guests for the main dinner, and a larger group invited only to the evening party. The couple look at each other and, after a pause, agree.\n\nNext is the venue. Nigora would like a garden by the river, but Mrs Ahmedova warns that the weather in September is unpredictable, and a tent would cost an extra two thousand pounds. Bobur prefers a restaurant in the city centre, which has a hall for a hundred and thirty people. The hall costs five thousand pounds, including food, which is a lot cheaper than the garden. Nigora is not completely convinced, but she accepts it because she cares more about the photographs than about the building. Mrs Ahmedova promises to ask the photographer whether he can take pictures in the park next to the restaurant.\n\nThe discussion turns to the budget. Their parents are contributing ten thousand pounds, and the couple have saved four thousand themselves. Mrs Ahmedova points out that this is enough for the dinner, music and flowers, but not for the honeymoon, which Bobur had also hoped to include. After some thought, Bobur says that they will postpone the honeymoon until the spring and go away for just a weekend now. Nigora is happy with the idea, although she says she would have chosen the beach.\n\nThen they come to the date. Originally, they wanted the fourteenth of September, but Mrs Ahmedova checks her diary and says that the restaurant is already booked on that day. The twenty-first is free. Bobur's brother cannot come on the twenty-first because he has an exam, but Bobur says that this cannot be helped. Finally, the planner asks whether there will be any speeches. Nigora says only one, by her grandfather, and she asks everyone to keep it short. They all laugh, and the meeting finishes with a list of tasks for next week.",
