topic: "A mentor's advice",
audioText: "Every Friday afternoon, Karim meets his mentor, a retired finance manager called Mrs Lawson, in a quiet cafe near his office. Karim is twenty-four and has just started his first proper job at a software company. This Friday he arrives looking worried, and before he has even ordered his tea, he tells her that he received his first salary and cannot understand where it all went.\n\nMrs Lawson laughs kindly and says that this is the most normal problem in the world. She asks him to take out his phone and look at his bank statement. Together they discover that Karim paid six hundred pounds for rent, two hundred and forty for food, and a surprising ninety for taxis, although his office is only fifteen minutes from his flat on foot. Karim admits that he took taxis because he was always late. Mrs Lawson does not criticise him. She simply says that getting up thirty minutes earlier is the cheapest investment he will ever make.\n\nThen she gives him three pieces of advice. The first is to pay himself first. On the day his salary arrives, he should move ten percent into a savings account before he spends anything. Karim says that ten percent seems a lot, and she answers that he can begin with five and increase it gradually, but he must begin. The second is to say yes to a pension scheme, which his company offers, and which will add money on top of his own. Karim says he thought pensions were for old people. She tells him that this is the biggest mistake of young workers, because time is what makes the money grow.\n\nThe third piece of advice is not about money at all. She tells him to ask for a performance review after six months and to prepare a list of everything he has achieved. Karim worries that asking for a raise might look greedy. Mrs Lawson says that it is not greedy, it is professional, though she warns him not to ask in the same week that he makes a mistake, which, she says, she learned the hard way.\n\nTowards the end, Karim mentions that his friend has offered him a share in a restaurant business, promising high profits. Mrs Lawson becomes serious. She says that she would not invest in anything that she did not understand or that a friend was pressing her to decide quickly. She suggests that he asks to see the accounts and talks to a lawyer first. Karim nods, writes it down, and promises to tell her what happens next Friday.",
