topic: "A talk by an exchange student",
audioText: "Hello, everyone. My name is Anvar, and I am going to talk for about ten minutes about the year I spent studying in Germany, which I did last year as part of an exchange programme. I know that many of you are thinking about applying for next year, and I hope that my experience will help you to decide.\n\nLet me begin with the money, because that is the first question people always ask. The programme gave me an allowance of four hundred and fifty euros a month. My rent was three hundred and eighty, so, as you can see, there was not much left. I had to find a part-time job, and I worked in a university library for ten hours a week. It was not exciting, but it improved my German more than any lesson. A word of warning, though. Some students think that the allowance is enough, and then they call their parents in the second month. Do not be one of them.\n\nThe second thing is the academic side. I had expected the teaching to be easier, because I was a guest, but it was quite the opposite. In Germany, lectures are only a small part of the course. You are expected to read a great deal independently and to take part in seminars, where the professors ask you challenging questions in front of everybody. In my first seminar, I understood perhaps half of what was said, and I said nothing at all. By December, I was arguing with my classmates, and it was one of my proudest moments.\n\nNext, living there. I shared a flat with three other students, one German, one Spanish and one from Brazil. At first, I was upset because we had so many arguments about cleaning. Then we made a timetable on the kitchen wall, and the problem disappeared, mostly. I also learned to cook, because I could not afford to eat in restaurants. My speciality became plov, which my flatmates adored, though they said it was too oily.\n\nWould I do it again? Definitely, although I would change a few things. I would learn more German before leaving, because the first month was exhausting. I would also travel less in the first term, as I spent a lot of money on trips and then had to study desperately during exams. And I would bring warmer clothes, because November is not like November here.\n\nFinally, the application. The deadline is the fifteenth of January. You need a letter, a recommendation from a professor, and evidence of your language level. If you have questions, please come to see me after the talk. I will be standing by the door, and I promise not to talk about plov.",
