topic: "Renting versus buying",
audioText: "Ilhom and his wife Dilfuza visit a financial adviser called Mr Clarke because they cannot decide whether to buy a flat or keep renting. They are both thirty-one, and they have a daughter who is almost three. Their savings amount to twenty-two thousand pounds, and their combined income is five thousand pounds a month before tax.\n\nMr Clarke begins by saying that the question is not as simple as most people believe. He knows that many families think that renting is throwing money away, but he does not agree. When you rent, he explains, you are paying for flexibility and freedom from repair costs, and there are times in life when that is worth the price. When you buy, you build up something that belongs to you, but you also take on risks, such as rising interest rates, repairs and the possibility that the price of the property falls.\n\nDilfuza asks how big a loan they could get. Mr Clarke says that most banks will lend about four times a yearly income, which in their case could be one hundred and forty thousand pounds if the bank considers their jobs secure. However, he adds that borrowing the maximum is rarely wise. He recommends keeping the monthly payment below thirty percent of their income, and that would mean about fifteen hundred pounds. Ilhom looks at his wife with concern, because that is nearly double their present rent.\n\nThen Mr Clarke talks about the deposit. Banks usually want ten percent of the price, but a larger deposit means lower interest. With twenty-two thousand pounds they could afford a flat costing two hundred thousand, but he warns that they should not use all their savings, because they also need money for legal fees, furniture and an emergency fund. Ilhom says that they had not thought about legal fees. Mr Clarke smiles and says that almost nobody does.\n\nHe then offers a different option. He suggests that they continue renting for two more years while they save an extra ten thousand pounds, and meanwhile watch the market. Ilhom thinks this is sensible, but Dilfuza is not sure. She is worried that prices might rise in the meantime, which would make the dream flat even more expensive. Mr Clarke admits that nobody can predict the market, and he would not tell them to wait if they could comfortably afford to buy now. But in their situation, he thinks, patience is cheaper than regret.\n\nAt the end, the couple say they will talk it over at home. Mr Clarke gives them a list of questions to discuss and a calculator on his website. He reminds them that his advice is free for the first meeting only. Dilfuza thanks him and says they will come back in a month, probably with more doubts than answers.",
