topic: "Lending money to a brother",
audioText: "Late on Saturday evening, Feruza sits at the kitchen table with her husband Oleg, and the conversation is not an easy one. Her younger brother Sherzod has asked to borrow three thousand pounds to open a small shop that sells mobile phone accessories. He was supposed to come for dinner tonight, but he cancelled at the last minute, and Feruza suspects that he is embarrassed.\n\nOleg says that he likes Sherzod, but he does not think they should lend the money. He reminds his wife that, two years ago, they lent him five hundred pounds for a motorbike, and he returned only half of it. Feruza answers that Sherzod was only twenty then, and he has changed. He now has a steady job at a car repair garage and has saved a thousand pounds himself. Oleg is not convinced and points out that the most likely reason for a new business to fail is not laziness but lack of experience, and Sherzod has never run a shop.\n\nFeruza looks tired and says that she does not want to ruin her relationship with her brother over money. Oleg replies that this is exactly the point, since lending to family can damage relationships much more than refusing. He tells her about his cousin, who lent money to a friend and has not spoken to him for six years. Feruza thinks this is an exaggeration, but she lowers her voice and agrees that the story is sad.\n\nAfter some silence, Oleg suggests a different approach. Instead of giving all three thousand pounds, they could lend one thousand five hundred and sign a simple written agreement, with monthly repayments of one hundred pounds beginning after six months. Feruza likes the idea, although she says it feels cold to ask a brother to sign a paper. Oleg says that it protects both sides, because nobody can later say that they misunderstood what was agreed.\n\nThen Feruza raises a practical point. Their own savings are meant for the new roof, and a loan of fifteen hundred would leave very little. Oleg says that he is willing to delay the roof until next autumn, as the old one has survived many winters. They also agree to ask Sherzod to show them a business plan, with expected costs and the rent of the shop.\n\nFinally, Feruza calls her brother, and he answers on the second ring. She tells him the plan and, to her relief, Sherzod is not offended. He says that he would have asked for a written agreement himself, and he will come over on Sunday to talk, and this time, he promises, he will not cancel.",
